28 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480628 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480628 #edb/ref 1077 "field-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480628 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480628 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "faf226ee-4861-5e14-8b25-2eece69e5941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2da83aef-8248-5784-87a2-78e988b82e95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480649 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=3x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x(1-2x)-xy)=3-12x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+4xy)=-3+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}3-12x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -3+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}3-12(0.5)-0 & -0.5 \\\\ 4(0) & -3+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -0.5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480663 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x)-0.8xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6036bb50-cd4e-590c-9c42-05fdc24940b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480663 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x)-0.8xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $1.5x(1-0.5x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-0.8xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5x(1-0.5x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f820e7d-e65e-5149-80bc-fca1cd9160a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480667 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -2y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.8).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.8).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480668 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480668 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480668 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=1.8x(1-0.1x)-0.7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4.5y+1.1xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "711de219-9297-5bb8-aae2-ee7607fb16cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480668 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.8x(1-0.1x)-0.7xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $1.8x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-0.7xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.8x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.8x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcc29b37-0226-5337-b5dc-f223113388b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480690 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=4-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+3xy)=-3+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}4-16x-y & -x \\\\ 3y & -3+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4-16(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 3(0) & -3+3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=x(1-0.2x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bee04d0f-21c8-5845-b5c1-0985dbd842b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480691 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=x(1-0.2x)-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1-0.2x)-2xy)=1-0.4x-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x(1-0.2x)-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+xy)=-3+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}1-0.4x-2y & -2x \\\\ y & -3+x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}1-0.4(0)-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 0 & -3+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480698 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480698 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x)-2.2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+2.8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da4c8dfe-99e9-5427-8a0d-f6ac8ce055dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480698 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x)-2.2xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $3.6x(1-0.02x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-2.2xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.6x(1-0.02x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=50.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $50.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.1x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f57e9bc6-8d1b-532c-906d-19c1ec051963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480707 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.1x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.1x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.1x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(10,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.1(2))-2(2)y & =0 \\\\ 4.8-4y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.2).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(10,0),\\,(2,1.2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
